ervice_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-28T21:37:26.139Z", "request_id": "req_011CgVMaqPXbKE2Js4Upzk3D", "thinking_duration_ms": 57628}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgVMaqmrWj6PbcpHTf3ke", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Candidate 5 gets the highest probability (0.24), with candidate 2 close behind (0.22). I worked this out in my head, not by simulation, and the two order schedules came out roughly even.\n\n**1. Ordering frequency.**\n- Plans 0, 5, 6 and 7 order every day for 26 days. That costs 26 × 24 = 624 in fixed order fees.\n- Plans 1, 2 and 4 order every other day, 13 times, for 312.\n- With the every-other-day schedule, the stock left in the evening after each delivery includes the next day's demand. For product 0 that is about 14 units × 0.5. For product 1 it is about 27 × 0.7. Over the whole horizon this extra holding comes to about 320.\n- So the every-other-day schedule saves 312 in fees and loses about 320 in holding. That is close to a tie.\n- Plan 3 orders every three days (216 in fees), but holds about 18 more per day than the daily schedule. That is roughly 450 of extra holding for only about 400 saved in fees. Product 1 also keeps only 4 days, so more of it risks expiring. It comes out behind.\n\n**2. How much above the forecast each plan orders.** I compared each order with the forecast demand for its delivery day.\n\n| Plans | Ratio to forecast |\n|---|---|\n| 6 and 4 | 1.0 |\n| 5 and 2 | about 1.1 |\n| 0 | about 1.15 |\n| 1 and 7 | about 1.2–1.25 |\n| 3 | 1.12 |\n\n**3. Which ratio is best.**\n- Ordering exactly the forecast (1.0) is not enough. Demand averages 1.046 × the forecast, and a missed unit of product 1 is expensive. It costs 9 in lost margin plus an 8 penalty.\n- Ordering too much is also costly. The plans are fixed in advance, so any surplus piles up and gets held. Product 1 then expires after 4 days, losing 5 per unit.\n- For product 1 the best ratio seems to be about 1.1–1.15. Product 0 can take a bit more, since it keeps 7 days and costs only 0.5 per day to hold.\n- That puts plans 5 and 2 in the lead, with 0 close behind. Plans 1 and 7 overstock. Plans 6 and 4 run short."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4351, "cache_read_input_tokens": 2305, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4351}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-28T21:37:34.304Z", "request_id": "req_011CgVMaqPXbKE2Js4Upzk3D"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 1}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 2, "content_block": {"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_016vSsGC2838c3fX6VdkUWfc", "name": "StructuredOutput", "input": {}, "caller": {"type": "direct"}}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgVMaqmrWj6PbcpHTf3ke", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_016vSsGC2838c3fX6VdkUWfc", "name": "StructuredOutput", "input": {"p": [0.18, 0.12, 0.22, 0.07, 0.05, 0.24, 0.05, 0.07]}, "caller": {"type": "direct"}}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "